### frankenscipy-9nw95 — THE MERGE KERNEL I OPTIMIZED IS UNREACHABLE IN THE DEFAULT BUILD

BlackThrush, 2026-08-24. This corrects my own reporting on this bead tonight.

## HOW IT SURFACED

The paired A/B instrument landed in 775d4a3e6 prints `merge_ab_dispatch_observed=` before any
timing and REFUSES the run if the library never consults the toggle. On its first invocation:

    merge_ab_dispatch_observed=false
    CONVECTION_SPLU_LIVE_FATAL the merge-kernel A/B toggle was never consulted: the two arms
    are the same code and no ratio from them is reportable

The two-arm probe control fired on its first use and refused to produce a number. Without it I
would have reported a paired ratio built from two arms that were the same code.

## THE FACT

    `apply_supernode_tails` has exactly ONE non-test caller:
        NativeSparseLu::factorize_csr_supernodal   (linalg.rs:3395)

    which is reached only here (linalg.rs:4740):
        let native = if SPLU_SUPERNODAL_ENABLE.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
            NativeSparseLu::factorize_csr_supernodal(...)
        } else {
            ... factorize_csr(...)
        };

    and:
        pub static SPLU_SUPERNODAL_ENABLE: PerfToggle = PerfToggle::new(false);   // DEFAULT OFF

So the merge kernel is dead code in the shipping configuration. The default factorization runs
`factorize_csr`, whose hot kernel is `apply_sorted_pivot_tail` — a different function with a
different signature (one pivot's tail, not a flat width x span buffer), already carrying its own
optimization history.

This is consistent with this bead's own closure, which says the supernodal line was kept
"in-tree behind default-off toggles (SPLU_SUPERNODAL_ENABLE), deliberately not deleted". I read
that sentence and did not connect it to the kernel I was working on.

## WHAT THAT DOES TO MY RESULTS ON THIS BEAD

STILL TRUE, as statements about that kernel — both counted under callgrind, both bit-identical,
both with driver tests:

  * b923646ab   inner loop   15.000 -> 12.010 Ir per element-update
  * 6c147ffd6   outer walk   18,194 -> 17,297 Ir per call

NOT TRUE, and I said it: that these could move the shipping cell. I wrote "the merge inner loop
is part of the factor, the factor is roughly two thirds of a factor-plus-16-solves job, so a
19.9% cut is worth plausibly a few percent". The premise is wrong. The merge kernel is 0% of the
default job, and the correct predicted end-to-end effect was EXACTLY ZERO, not "a few percent".

That retroactively explains both end-to-end rows, and I am withdrawing the reading I gave the
first one:

  * 0.663058 -> 0.713729, CIs disjoint, nulls clean. I called this "consistent with the count"
    and attributed ~1.7% to the lever. THAT ATTRIBUTION IS WITHDRAWN. The lever cannot have
    contributed anything. The move was run-to-run variation, which is exactly what the
    across-run caveat I attached to it warned about — the caveat was right and my attribution
    ignored my own caveat.
  * 0.713729 -> 0.700061, control null biased, statistics disagreeing in sign. Correctly
    classified as a NON-CLAIM at the time; now explained rather than merely flagged.

## WHAT IS STILL WORTH SOMETHING

  * The instrument. The paired toggle, the bit-identity driver across all three code paths, and
    the dispatch refusal are correct and are what caught this. That refusal is now a permanent
    guard on the convection arm.
  * The counted method. The bar this bead set for a successor — Ir per element-update — remains
    the right currency, and the cost decomposition (per call = constant + slope x elements) is
    reusable on any kernel.
  * A REJECT that is now sharper: the supernodal path's merge kernel is 1.05x cheaper than it
    was, so the dense-scatter design this bead rejected is now measured against a FASTER
    baseline. That widens the gap that made it a REJECT rather than narrowing it.

## WHAT SHOULD HAPPEN NEXT, and what should NOT

SHOULD NOT: further optimization of `apply_supernode_tails` on the theory that it helps the
product. It cannot, while `SPLU_SUPERNODAL_ENABLE` is false.

SHOULD: any future splu factor lever targets `apply_sorted_pivot_tail` and the `factorize_csr`
path, and any harness A/B for it must carry the same dispatch proof this one does — because the
lesson here is that a kernel can look hot in a microbenchmark and be unreachable in production,
and only a dispatch check distinguishes those.

NOT REVERTED: the two counted levers stay. They are bit-identical, tested, and strictly less
work on code that is still in the tree and still reachable when the toggle is enabled. Reverting
would be churn with no benefit. What changes is the CLAIM attached to them, which is this file.

